            <ArticleTitle>Not as skinny as we used to think: Body mass index in children and adolescents at diagnosis of type 1 diabetes mellitus.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>292-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jdiacomp.2015.11.022</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1056-8727(15)00460-2</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>This retrospective analysis of clinical data for children (2-18 years old) with incident T1D found surprisingly low (9%) prevalence of underweight, and high (15% overweight; 8% obesity) respectively at diagnosis. These results suggests a need to rethink the classic clinical teaching surrounding skinnier presentation at diagnosis, and importantly, the corresponding expectation of weight gain after insulin therapy initiation.</AbstractText>
